---
intent: >
  Add a `kind?: "orientation" | "config"` discriminator to the `aide_brain`
  tool — closed two-value vocabulary, default `"orientation"`, schema-level
  rejection of unknown values, in-memory dispatch on parseBrainAide's typed
  result, identical non-ok remediation prose regardless of `kind`, and
  `{ status, instructions }` response shape preserved exactly. Update the
  registered MCP tool description in `src/index.ts` to teach the new
  parameter and its vocabulary. Update tests to cover both `kind` values
  for `ok`, kind-agnostic non-ok branches, and rejection of every seed
  spelling.
---

## Project Structure

This plan is scoped to `src/tools/brain/`. The module is two files plus a
co-located spec; no new files are introduced. Two files outside this scope
need touch-up edits — listed in `## Out-of-scope edits` below.

```
src/tools/brain/                            ← scope root for this plan
├── .aide                                   ← intent spec (already exists, body just updated)
├── plan.aide                               ← this file
├── todo.aide                               ← prior alignment-cycle log (untouched)
├── index.ts                                ← orchestrator (already exists, MODIFIED)
│                                              export const BrainInput: ZodObject
│                                                with optional `kind` enum field
│                                              export default async function brain(
│                                                root: string,
│                                                input?: { kind?: "orientation" | "config" }
│                                              ): Promise<BrainToolResult>
└── index.test.ts                           ← Vitest test file (already exists, MODIFIED)
                                              covers ok×{orientation,config}, non-ok×{both kinds},
                                              defensive fallback, schema rejects every seed spelling
```

## Out-of-scope edits

Two files outside `src/tools/brain/` need edits to keep this tool's contract
in lockstep with its callers. The implementor handles them inside the
relevant numbered steps below — not as a separate plan.

- `src/index.ts` — the tool registration site. Two edits: (a) the
  `inputSchema` for `aide_brain` in `ListToolsRequestSchema` gains a
  `kind` property with `enum: ["orientation", "config"]` and a description
  that documents default + vocabulary; (b) the registered `description`
  string for `aide_brain` is rewritten to teach the new `kind` parameter
  and its vocabulary, the orientation/config split, the live-vs-seed rule,
  and to drop pre-rework language ("verbatim prose body"). The tool
  dispatch case `aide_brain` also forwards the parsed input to the
  handler: `brain(root, BrainInput.parse(args))` instead of discarding the
  parsed object.
- `src/types/index.ts` — `BrainToolResult` already has the right shape
  (`{ status, instructions }`). No structural changes needed; just confirm
  no `kind` echo is added on the wire (negative requirement, enforced by
  tests). The `ParseBrainAideResult` `ok` variant must already carry the
  rework-era named fields (`orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`,
  `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) by the time this
  plan runs — see Prerequisites.

## Prerequisites

- `parseBrainAide` (at `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts`) must export
  the rework-era 6-section typed `ok` variant — its `ok` result must
  carry `orientation: string` and `config: string` as separately-named
  fields, alongside `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
  `researchIndex`. This plan reads `parseResult.orientation` and
  `parseResult.config` directly; it does not parse markers itself.
- `buildBrainState` (at `src/service/buildBrainState/index.ts`) continues
  to return the existing `BrainState` tagged union (`ok`,
  `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`). No contract change here
  — this plan forwards the union through unchanged.
- The pre-rework typed-key mapping (`prose`, `playbook`, `research`)
  MUST already be gone from `parseBrainAide`'s `ok` variant. If those
  keys still exist when this plan runs, the parser-side rename is
  incomplete and this plan stops.

## Plan

### 1. Tighten `BrainInput` schema and handler signature for the `kind` discriminator

Read: `coding-playbook/api/validation`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] 1a. In `src/tools/brain/index.ts`, redefine `BrainInput` as a Zod
      object with a single optional field `kind` whose Zod type is
      `z.enum(["orientation", "config"]).optional()`. Do NOT add a
      `.default("orientation")` on the schema — the handler resolves
      the default after parsing so a missing field is unambiguous to
      the framework's input-validation layer (default would imply the
      schema itself coerced an absent value to a present one, blurring
      the "schema rejects unknown values" framing). The schema's job
      is to enforce vocabulary; the handler's job is to resolve the
      default.
- [x] 1b. Define a module-scope type alias at the top of the file
      (above the prose constants), e.g. `type BrainKind = "orientation"
      | "config";`. The handler's exhaustive switch types against this
      alias and a future contributor adding a new value must update
      both the schema and the alias to compile.
- [x] 1c. Change the handler signature to `export default async
      function brain(root: string, input?: { kind?: BrainKind }):
      Promise<BrainToolResult>`. The second parameter is optional so
      the existing single-arg call sites (in tests) keep compiling;
      resolve the effective kind once at the top of the body as
      `const kind: BrainKind = input?.kind ?? "orientation";`. Resolve
      it BEFORE the `buildBrainState` call so the resolved value is
      available for the `ok` branch — non-ok branches do not consume
      it but resolving once keeps control flow linear.
- [x] 1d. Update the file-level JSDoc above `brain` to describe the
      new contract: optional `kind` parameter with two-value
      vocabulary, default `"orientation"`, in-memory dispatch on
      parseBrainAide's named fields, identical non-ok remediation
      prose regardless of `kind`. Drop the pre-rework "verbatim prose
      body" phrasing — the JSDoc must use the `orientation` /
      `config` vocabulary now.
- [x] 1e. Drop or rewrite any inline comments below the handler that
      still reference `parseResult.prose`. The comment block above
      the defensive fallback (Step 4 in the existing comments) stays,
      but its reference to "the prose" becomes "the selected
      section" since either field could be the one that came back
      ok-but-via-fallback.

### 2. Rewrite the `ok` branch to dispatch on `kind` against parseBrainAide's typed result

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`

- [x] 2a. After the three non-ok early returns, call
      `parseBrainAide(root)` exactly once (no change). On
      `parseResult.kind === "ok"`, replace the existing
      `parseResult.prose` access with a switch on the module-scope
      `kind` value. The pipeline is: read parseResult once → switch
      on `kind` → return `{ status: "ok", instructions:
      parseResult.orientation }` for `"orientation"` and
      `{ status: "ok", instructions: parseResult.config }` for
      `"config"`. No third branch.
- [x] 2b. Use the TypeScript exhaustiveness pattern. Either: a switch
      whose `default` branch contains `const _exhaustive: never =
      kind;` and falls through to the defensive fallback so a runtime
      schema-bypass still maps to a non-empty `instructions`; or an
      `if/else if` chain with a final `_exhaustive: never` assertion
      below it. Pick whichever shape compiles cleanly without
      `eslint-disable`; the type checker is the load-bearing
      guarantee — if `BrainKind` gains a value, the switch must
      fail to compile until the new branch is wired.
- [x] 2c. The existing defensive fallback below the `ok` branch
      (`parseResult.kind` non-`ok` despite `buildBrainState` saying
      `ok`) stays unchanged in behavior — it still routes to
      `NO_BRAIN_AIDE_PROSE` on `status: "no-brain-aide"`. The
      fallback is `kind`-agnostic by design (the file is broken for
      both sections in the same way). Update the inline comment
      above the fallback so it no longer says "the prose"; it says
      "the selected section" or equivalent.

### 3. Confirm non-ok branches stay byte-identical regardless of `kind`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] 3a. The three non-ok branches (`no-brain-aide`,
      `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`) keep their existing fixed
      remediation prose constants (`NO_BRAIN_AIDE_PROSE`,
