## Decisions

- **Schema-level vocabulary, default in handler.** The Zod schema
  uses `z.enum([...]).optional()` rather than
  `.default("orientation")` so the framework's input-validation
  layer cleanly distinguishes "absent" from "present and
  accepted." The handler does the null-coalescing-to-default. This
  matches the spec's Strategy paragraph "Schema-level rejection of
  unknown `kind` values, not handler-level" — the schema's job is
  vocabulary enforcement; the handler's job is dispatch and default
  resolution. A `.default()` on the schema would technically work
  but blurs the responsibility line and makes test fixtures harder
  to reason about (Zod fills in the default before the handler
  sees the input, so a test asserting "schema parse of `{}`
  returns `{ kind: undefined }`" would assert the wrong thing).
- **Module-scope `BrainKind` alias for the exhaustive switch.**
  Defined alongside the schema rather than inferred from the Zod
  schema's output type. Inferring (`z.infer<typeof
  BrainInput>["kind"]`) would also work but the alias is shorter,
  the spec's vocabulary lives in one place, and the type checker
  enforces the same exhaustiveness guarantee. A future contributor
  adding a new value updates two places (the schema + the alias),
  and the handler's switch fails to compile until the new branch
  is wired — exactly the constraint the spec's Strategy paragraph
  "schema-level enforcement of the live-vs-seed split" requires.
- **Handler signature gains an optional second parameter rather
  than refactoring to a single options object.** The existing
  signature is `(root: string)` — changing to `(root: string,
  input?: { kind? })` preserves call-site compatibility (no caller
  pass-through breaks) while still making the parameter typed. The
  MCP registration site in `src/index.ts` is the only real caller;
  the test file calls the handler directly and gains a typed
  second argument cleanly. A full refactor to `(opts: { root,
  kind? })` would touch the registration site and every test more
  invasively for no design benefit.
- **Defensive fallback target stays `no-brain-aide`.** The
  pre-rework defensive-fallback test asserts this; the spec's
  Strategy paragraph "A defensive fallback guards the
  'instructions never empty' invariant" pins the target as
  `no-brain-aide` for the missing-or-broken-file shape that
  "buildBrainState says ok but parser disagrees" represents. No
  change.
- **Tests use `it.each` over the (state × kind) matrix where it
  reduces duplication, but stay split where the assertion text is
  meaningfully different per branch.** The "non-empty
  instructions" test is a pure-matrix shape — perfect for
  `it.each`. The "verbatim bytes per kind" tests stay as separate
  `it()` blocks because the fixture data and the assertion target
  differ per kind, and reading them at code-review time should
  not require unwinding a parameter table. Source: spec's
  Good-examples blocks, which list distinct Good-example
  responses per kind.
- **MCP description rewrite is in-scope for this plan.** The
  spec's desired outcome on "the MCP tool description registered
  for `aide_brain` … is an auditable contract surface" makes the
  description a falsifiable surface. Drift between the description
  and the handler's runtime behavior is a QA failure traceable to
  this outcome, so the description rewrite must land in the same
  build as the handler change. The implementor handles
  `src/index.ts` Step 4 inside this plan rather than escalating to
  a separate plan.
- **No `BrainToolResult` type changes.** The wire shape is
  `{ status, instructions }` and stays that way. The
  negative-shape tests (no `backend`, no `name`, no `connector`,
  no echoed `kind`) enforce this; no type-level enforcement is
  added because the existing `BrainToolResult` already constrains
  the response shape (and adding a `kind` echo to the type would
  be a code change, not a wire-level concern).
- **One `parseBrainAide` call per `ok` invocation, in-memory
  dispatch on `kind`.** The spec's Strategy paragraph "One
  `parseBrainAide` call per `ok` invocation; section selection is
  in-memory dispatch" pins this. Re-parsing per `kind` would
  double I/O for no benefit and create a between-reads
  inconsistency window. The Bad-examples block explicitly shows
  the wrong shape (two parses); the plan's switch happens after
  the single parse call.
- **Test fixtures supply every named `ok`-variant field, even
  unused ones.** The rework-era `ParseBrainAideResult["ok"]`
  carries six fields (`orientation`, `config`, `playbookIndex`,
  `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`); a fixture
  that omits any of them fails to type-check against the parser's
  contract. Tests supply placeholder strings for the four unused
  seed fields. This is purely a TypeScript-conformance
  consideration; no test asserts anything about the seed-field
  values, since the spec's outcomes guarantee those fields are
  never returned by this tool.
